Architecture Overview
graph.py acts as the structural spine. It takes the flat, parsed engineering data objects and converts them into an in-memory Directed Multigraph Knowledge Graph using NetworkX.

A Directed Multigraph Knowledge Graph is a knowledge graph where:
Directed → relationships have a specific direction.
Multigraph → two nodes can have multiple different edges/relationships between them.
Knowledge graph → the nodes and relationships represent real-world entities and their semantic relationships.

builds precise structural links across three distinct tiers of project documentation: Contract clauses -> Spec Requirements -> Design Callouts


NetworkX
A standard Python library for creating, manipulating, and studying complex network structures, graphs, and node/edge relationships.

Directed Graph
A graph where relationships have direction.

Closed-form vs. Unstructured Triples
Closed-form (numeric): If a contract clause, spec requirement, and drawing callout all contain explicit numerical parameters/tolerances, the triple is classified as numeric and routed to the deterministic engine.   Unstructured (free_text): If any element in the triple involves unstructured text or qualitative descriptions, the triple is marked as free_text and routed to the agentic LLM fallback. 


triples: Active triples ready for verification.   
excluded: Triples associated with obsolete/superseded drawing revisions.   gaps: Unresolved clauses that lack corresponding specs or drawing callouts.